(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-dermatological-infestations-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Dermatological Infestations Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"How is cutaneous larva migrans treated?","choices":["Surgical excision or cryotherapy of the advancing edge of the lesion under local anaesthesia","Doxycycline 100 mg by mouth twice daily for a course of ten to fourteen days","Albendazole 400 mg by mouth daily for three days, or ivermectin 200 micrograms per kilogram for one to two days","Topical permethrin applied overnight to the whole skin surface with a repeat application at one week"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — topical therapy is less effective and surgical excision or cryotherapy is not recommended. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which living circumstances predispose to body louse infestation?","choices":["Working outdoors in hot humid weather with occlusive clothing","Homelessness, displacement by war or disaster, and crowded conditions with poor hygiene","Regular use of shared hot tubs, whirlpools and public water slides in summer","Frequent international travel with stays in hotels and hostels"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the inability to wash or change clothes is what allows it to persist. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"How is Rocky Mountain spotted fever treated, including in pregnancy and young children?","choices":["Amoxicillin for children and pregnant patients, with doxycycline reserved for adults only","Azithromycin for all patients, since doxycycline is contraindicated below eight years of age","Intravenous ceftriaxone for all patients, with oral therapy reserved for prophylaxis","Doxycycline for adults, pregnant patients and children, with desensitisation where it is contraindicated"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — 100 mg twice daily for adults and 2.2 mg per kilogram twice daily for children, for 5 to 10 days. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"How are nits distinguished from dandruff, and what does each finding indicate?","choices":["Nits are found only at the hair tips; finding them anywhere along the shaft excludes infestation","Nits cannot be removed from the hair shaft; live lice indicate active infestation while nits indicate past or present","Nits brush off the hair shaft easily; live lice indicate past infestation while nits indicate active disease","Nits fluoresce under a Wood's lamp; their presence alone confirms that the infestation is still active"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — viable eggs are tan to brown and hatched remains are clear, white or light. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"In which patients might scabies excoriations be seen along the head and neck?","choices":["Infants, the elderly and the immunocompromised","Adolescents and young adults in shared housing","Pregnant patients in their third trimester","Patients who have already been treated once"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — healthy adults have head and neck sparing. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What is the prognosis and treatment of a hobo spider bite?","choices":["Wounds heal within several weeks and headaches may last a week; treatment is supportive","Wounds require surgical reconstruction and healing takes months; treatment is operative","Wounds resolve within hours; treatment is calcium gluconate with muscle relaxants","Wounds resolve within days; treatment is a ten-day course of oral doxycycline"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — death from severe systemic effects, including aplastic anaemia, is rare. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"What characterises stage 2, early disseminated Lyme disease?","choices":["One week after the bite, with a single expanding lesion and constitutional symptoms","Months to years later, with monoarticular or oligoarticular arthritis of weight-bearing joints","Within thirty minutes of the bite, with sweating, piloerection and muscle cramping","Days to weeks later, involving skin, central nervous system, heart, musculoskeletal system and eyes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — cranial nerve palsies, meningitis, radiculopathies, arthralgias and stiff neck. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"How does a black widow spider bite present within the first thirty minutes?","choices":["Localised erythema, piloerection and sweating around the bite, then crampy abdominal pain and muscle spasm","A painless bite with induration and paraesthesia at the site, then a large area of erythema around it","A central violaceous area ringed by blanched skin and then a large asymmetric red area","Immediate burning and pain with local erythema, swelling and urticaria at the site"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — headache, paraesthesia, nausea, vomiting, hypertension and seizures may follow. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"When is serological testing for Lyme disease most useful?","choices":["In patients from endemic regions, in whom a negative result excludes the diagnosis","In patients who do not live in an endemic region and present with signs possibly consistent with it","In every patient who reports a tick bite, regardless of whether any rash has developed","In patients with erythema migrans, in whom the result determines whether to treat"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a patient with erythema migrans is diagnosed and treated clinically. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"How does the Rocky Mountain spotted fever rash spread?","choices":["It starts in the interdigital webs and wrists and spreads to the axillae and genitalia","It starts on the ankles and wrists and spreads centripetally over six to eighteen hours, involving palms and soles but sparing the face","It starts on the trunk and spreads outward centrifugally over several days, sparing the palms and the soles entirely","It starts at the site of the bite as a single expanding lesion with a clear centre"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — blanching macules or papules that may become petechial and purpuric. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"What does cerebrospinal fluid show in Rocky Mountain spotted fever?","choices":["Leukocytosis with markedly low glucose and very high protein","Entirely normal cell count, protein and glucose in every case","Eosinophilia with raised immunoglobulin E in the fluid","Leukocytosis with moderately raised protein and normal glucose"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — blood shows thrombocytopenia, anaemia, mild hyponatraemia and transaminitis. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"Which patients are at increased risk of complications from a black widow bite, and how is envenomation treated?","choices":["Pregnant patients and children under eight years of age; treat with doxycycline given through a graded desensitisation protocol","Immunosuppressed patients and the elderly; treat with ivermectin every two weeks for three doses","Patients with a positive skin test; treat with subcutaneous epinephrine and desensitisation therapy","The very old, the very young and those with cardiovascular disease; treat with calcium gluconate, narcotics, muscle relaxants and benzodiazepines"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and tetanus vaccination status must be up to date. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What is the position on prophylactic antibiotics after a tick bite in a Rocky Mountain spotted fever area?","choices":["Prophylactic antibiotic therapy is not recommended","A single dose of doxycycline is recommended for every tick bite","A five-day course of doxycycline is recommended in endemic areas","Prophylaxis is recommended only for children under ten years of age"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — prevention rests on avoiding exposure, protective clothing, tick checks and DEET. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"Which physical methods are described for head lice, and what is their limitation?","choices":["Weekly application of an occlusive agent such as mayonnaise or mineral oil to smother the live lice and eggs","Head shaving, or combing nits out after two minutes of hair moisturiser every few days; effective but slow and painful","Head shaving alone, which is completely curative and requires no further treatment at all","Daily hot air drying of the scalp, which kills both the live lice and the attached eggs"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — they need adjuvant therapy alongside them. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which long-term measures are offered after a sting-induced anaphylactic reaction?","choices":["Carry an epinephrine auto-injector, and desensitisation therapy where the skin test is positive","Carry oral antihistamines, with a short course of prednisolone kept for emergencies","Carry a topical corticosteroid, with local anaesthetic injection for pain control","Carry oral doxycycline, to be started at the first sign of any systemic symptoms"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — immunotherapy is specified for fire ant hypersensitivity. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"How do tarantulas cause human disease, and how is it managed?","choices":["Injected cytotoxin causes central necrosis and eschar; treat with delayed surgical reconstruction","A bite causes painless induration and vesicles; treat supportively over several weeks","Shed hairs embed in skin or eyes causing pruritus, granulomas or conjunctivitis; treat with topical steroid and refer eyes to ophthalmology","Injected neurotoxin causes muscle spasm and abdominal pain; treat with calcium gluconate, narcotics and benzodiazepines"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — symptoms are generally mild and local. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"What causes cercarial dermatitis, also called swimmer's itch?","choices":["Penetration of the skin by an adult female flea that then lays her eggs within it","Penetration of the stratum corneum by a mite that tunnels through it as it moves","Penetration of the skin by the cercarial larval form of certain parasitic flatworms","Penetration of the skin by the larva of an animal hookworm from contaminated soil"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the cercaria passes from an intermediate host such as a snail to another host. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"Which patients are at particular risk in facility-associated scabies?","choices":["Homeless people and refugees living in crowded conditions","Travellers staying in hotels and using second-hand furniture","Elderly and immunosuppressed residents of long-term care facilities","Children between three and twelve years in school settings"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a hospital epidemic can follow their admission. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What draws bedbugs out to feed, and where do they hide during the day?","choices":["Perspiration and body odour draw them out; they burrow into the stratum corneum of the skin","Blood on the skin surface draws them out; they live permanently attached to the hair shaft","Warmth and carbon dioxide draw them out; they hide in cracks and crevices of headboards and picture frames","Light and movement draw them out; they hide in the seams of clothing worn close to the skin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — they also hide behind loose wallpaper and in other dark places. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Why is a multimodal approach recommended for pediculosis?","choices":["Because of increasing resistance, with pediculicidal effect read twenty-four hours after application","Because the infestation always recurs within a month regardless of the treatment used","Because no single treatment is licensed for children under twelve years of age","Because nits cannot be killed by any chemical agent currently available anywhere"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — that reading interval is the World Health Organization recommendation. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Where is the hobo spider found and what is it often mistaken for?","choices":["The American Midwest and Southeast, where it is the predominant necrotic spider and is mistaken for the tarantula","All but the most northern states, where it is mistaken for the black widow spider","The Great Lakes region, where it is mistaken for a harmless house spider entirely","The Pacific Northwest, where it is the predominant cause of necrotic arachnidism and is mistaken for the brown recluse"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it is brown with a grey herringbone pattern and bites from July to September. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which caterpillar is described as the most poisonous, and what does its sting produce?","choices":["The tarantula, producing pruritus and granulomatous reactions from embedded hairs","The asp or puss caterpillar, producing an intensely painful sting and train-track purpura","The gypsy moth caterpillar, producing pruritic papules arranged in linear streaks","The processionary caterpillar, producing urticaria, angio-oedema and anaphylaxis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The gypsy moth caterpillar causes erucism and processionary caterpillars cause anaphylaxis. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which two flea families are named, and what does each cause?","choices":["Pulicidae causes tungiasis; Tungidae includes the rat and cat fleas that transmit plague","Cimicidae causes tungiasis; Pediculidae includes the fleas transmitting endemic typhus","Sarcoptidae causes tungiasis; Theraphosidae includes the fleas transmitting plague","Tungidae causes tungiasis; Pulicidae includes the rat and cat fleas that transmit plague and typhus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — fleas jump to a height of about 18 centimetres. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"How is a necrotic brown recluse wound managed?","choices":["Calcium gluconate with muscle relaxants and benzodiazepines given intravenously on arrival","Albendazole for three days with repeated courses if the lesion has not resolved","Pain control, warm compresses and avoiding strenuous exercise, with surgery delayed until the wound is stable","Immediate wide surgical excision of the affected area followed by reconstruction and grafting"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — antibiotics are for secondary bacterial infection, and healing is very slow. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Which conditions are named in the differential for scabies?","choices":["Bullous pemphigoid, pemphigus, thermal burn and herpes zoster infection","Atopic dermatitis, body and pubic lice, other arthropod bites, dermatitis herpetiformis, fungal infection and psoriasis","Cellulitis, erysipelas, necrotizing fasciitis and a deep vein thrombosis of the whole affected lower leg","Lentigo maligna, seborrheic keratosis, café-au-lait macule and freckles"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — all can produce an intensely itchy eruption. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"How should a honeybee sting be removed and why?","choices":["Scrape it off with a card edge or dull knife held parallel to the skin, as fast as possible","Grasp it with fine forceps and pull it straight out with steady traction on the shaft","Leave it in place, since removing it releases the remaining venom into the tissue","Apply ice for twenty minutes first, then remove it once the swelling has settled"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the barbed ovipositor stays impaled and continues to pump venom. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"How do primary and secondary skin lesions differ in the depth they affect?","choices":["Primary lesions arise from infection while secondary lesions arise from an immune reaction to that infection","Primary lesions appear first in time while secondary lesions appear only after treatment has been started","Primary lesions affect the epidermis and superficial dermis; secondary lesions infiltrate the dermis or subcutaneous tissue","Primary lesions infiltrate the dermis and subcutaneous tissue; secondary lesions affect only the superficial epidermis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Their combination determines the diagnostic category, also called the reaction pattern. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Why is facility-associated scabies a particular concern?","choices":["Residents travel frequently, carrying the organism between facilities in luggage and bedding","Residents are elderly and immunosuppressed, and a hospital epidemic can follow their admission","Residents are children under twelve, in whom the infestation spreads through shared headgear","Residents are homeless or displaced, in whom infestation persists through unwashed clothing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it is difficult to eradicate once healthcare workers are infested. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"In whom is body louse infestation typically found, and how does it persist?","choices":["Schoolchildren sharing hats and hair accessories, persisting through repeated close head contact","Sexually active adults across all social levels, often with a concurrent sexually transmitted infection","Long-term care residents who are elderly and immunosuppressed, persisting through staff carriage","Homeless people, refugees, war victims and those in crowded conditions, persisting via unwashed clothing"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — transmission is through contaminated clothing and bedding. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What is the time course of cercarial dermatitis after exposure?","choices":["Urticaria-like lesions and prickling for about 30 minutes, severe itch at 10 to 12 hours, papules within 24 hours, peaking at 48 to 72 hours","Pruritus first appearing four to six weeks after exposure, then insidious and severe nocturnal itching that persists for months","Fever for the first three days followed by a rash two to four days after the fever begins","A single lesion appearing about one week after exposure and expanding steadily thereafter"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — papules progress to vesicles and pustules, sometimes with headache, fever and lymphangitis. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 90"}]};
